Writing Desktop Applications in JavaScript<P>
<HR>
<H1>17  Do it yourself: a text area</H1>
<HR>
The Organiser's notepad pages are typed into with the desktop's <CODE>TextArea</CODE>: several lines of text in
a window, with a caret, word wrap and scrolling. The Wimp itself only has writable icons, which hold one line.
This last chapter builds a text area of your own, <CODE>TextBox</CODE>, and puts it in the Notepad in place
of the desktop's. You won't need to use it - the desktop's is better - but by the end you'll know how such a
thing works, which is the best way to use one well, and the way to build other things the desktop doesn't
have.<P>
<P><CENTER><IMG SRC="PICS/O17TextBox" WIDTH=910 HEIGHT=569></CENTER>
The example is <CODE>!Org17</CODE>: the finished organiser with one module added, <CODE>TextBox</CODE>, and two
lines changed in <CODE>NotesPage</CODE>.<P>

<A NAME=S1></A>
<H2>What a text area has to do</H2>
Plan it as the Organiser was planned, by what it remembers and what happens:<P>
<DL>
<DT>It remembers
<DD>the text (one string, with <CODE>'\n'</CODE> between lines), where the caret is (an index into the
text), and how far it has scrolled.<P>
<DT>It draws
<DD>the text, broken into lines that fit its width, on a canvas of its own in the window; and it shows the
Wimp's caret at the right place.<P>
<DT>It responds to
<DD>a click inside it (the caret goes there), and keys while it has the caret: characters are typed in,
Backspace takes one away, Return starts a new line, the arrow keys move about.<P>
</DL>
Everything else - the window, its other clicks and keys - belongs to the program, so the text area must take
only what's meant for it.<P>

<A NAME=S2></A>
<H2>The text box and its canvas</H2>
<PRE>
// TextBox - several lines of text you can type into, drawn by the
// program itself: a small version of the desktop's TextArea. It has
// its own canvas in the window's work area, wraps words to its width,
// scrolls to keep the caret in view, and uses the Wimp's caret.
//
//   const box = new TextBox(w, { x, y, w, h, text, font })
//   box.text                 the text ('\n' between lines)
//   box.on('change', ({ text }) =&gt; ...)   after each edit
//   box.focus(), box.focused, box.insert(s), box.remove()
import { wimp } from 'riscos';
import { Emitter } from './Toolkit/Emitter';

const PAD = 4, LINE = 18;

export class TextBox extends Emitter {
  constructor(win, { x, y, w, h, text = '',
    font = '14px Homerton' }) {
    super();
    Object.assign(this, { win, x, y, w, h, font });
    this.text = text;
    this.caret = text.length;          // where typing goes
    this.scroll = 0;                   // how far it has scrolled
    this.focused = false;
    const c = this.canvas = document.createElement('canvas');
    c.width = w; c.height = h;
    Object.assign(c.style, { position: 'absolute', left: `${x}px`,
      top: `${y}px`, pointerEvents: 'none' });
    win.work.appendChild(c);
    this.g = c.getContext('2d');
    // our handlers go first: the window only sees what we don't use
    this.offs = [
      win.on('click', (ev) =&gt; this.click(ev), { first: true }),
      win.on('key', (ev) =&gt; this.key(ev), { first: true }),
      win.on('losecaret', () =&gt; { this.focused = false; }),
    ];
    this.draw();
  }
</PRE>
The constructor keeps the box's position and size, and the text. <CODE>Object.assign(this, { win, x, y,
... })</CODE> (chapter 2) sets several properties in one go.<P>
The box draws on a canvas of its own. <CODE>document.createElement('canvas')</CODE> makes one, as it did for
printing in chapter 16, but this time the canvas goes <I>into</I> the window: its <CODE>style</CODE> says
where it's shown, at <CODE>x</CODE>, <CODE>y</CODE>, and <CODE>appendChild</CODE> puts it in
<CODE>win.work</CODE>, the window's work area - the desktop's way of putting a canvas of your own in a
window, where it scrolls with the work area. Its
<CODE>pointerEvents: 'none'</CODE> lets clicks go through it to the window, which gives them to the program as
usual.<P>

<H3>First in the queue</H3>
The window's events are the next problem. <CODE>win.on('click', ...)</CODE> adds a handler to the end of the
window's list, and the handlers are called in turn until one returns <CODE>true</CODE>. The organiser's own
click handler was added long before any text box was made, and it takes every click - so a text box added
after it would never see one.<P>
<CODE>{ first: true }</CODE>, which chapter 14 showed the desktop's TextArea using, puts a handler at the
<I>front</I> of the list instead. The text box's handlers
run first; they return <CODE>true</CODE> for a click inside the box, or a key while it has the caret, and
<CODE>undefined</CODE> for anything else, which then goes on to the organiser's handlers as before.<P>
<CODE>win.on</CODE> returns a function that takes the handler away again, and the box keeps them all in
<CODE>offs</CODE>. That matters: a notepad page's box is thrown away when you turn to another page, and a
box that left its handlers behind would go on taking clicks and keys for a text that isn't there any more.<P>
<PRE>
  focus() { this.focused = true; this.draw(); }
  remove() {
    for (const off of this.offs) off();
    this.canvas.remove();
    this.focused = false;
  }
</PRE>

<A NAME=S3></A>
<H2>Lines that fit</H2>
The text is one string; the box shows it as lines. <CODE>lines()</CODE> works them out, as a list of where
each line starts and ends in the text:<P>
<PRE>
  /** The lines as drawn: [{ start, end }] indexes into the text. */
  lines() {
    const g = this.g, t = this.text, max = this.w - 2 * PAD;
    g.font = this.font;
    const out = [];
    let start = 0;
    while (start &lt;= t.length) {
      let end = t.indexOf('\n', start);
      if (end &lt; 0) end = t.length;
      // too wide? break at the last space that fits
      while (g.measureText(t.slice(start, end)).width &gt; max) {
        const sp = t.lastIndexOf(' ', end - 1);
        end = sp &gt; start ? sp : end - 1;
      }
      out.push({ start, end });
      start = t[end] === '\n' || t[end] === ' ' ? end + 1 : end;
      if (end &gt;= t.length) break;
    }
    return out.length ? out : [{ start: 0, end: 0 }];
  }
</PRE>
It takes the text up to the next <CODE>'\n'</CODE>. If that's too wide - <CODE>measureText</CODE> says how
wide text is in the canvas's font - it looks for the last space before the end and tries again, and so on
until the line fits. (A single word too long for the box is cut wherever it fits.) The next line starts after
the newline or space it broke at.<P>
This is called whenever the box is drawn or a key is pressed. Measuring the text each time is slow in
principle, but a notepad page is small, and it keeps the program simple: the lines are never out of date.<P>
<CODE>where()</CODE> finds the caret's line and how far across it the caret is, in pixels:<P>
<PRE>
  /** The line the caret is on, and how far along it is. */
  where(lines = this.lines()) {
    let n = lines.findLastIndex((l) =&gt; l.start &lt;= this.caret);
    if (n &lt; 0) n = 0;
    const l = lines[n];
    this.g.font = this.font;
    const x = this.g.measureText(this.text.slice(l.start,
      this.caret)).width;
    return { n, x };
  }
</PRE>
<CODE>findLastIndex</CODE> is to <CODE>findIndex</CODE> what <CODE>findLast</CODE> (chapter 10) is to
<CODE>find</CODE>: it looks from the end of the array, and gives the index of the first item it comes to that
passes the test - here the last line starting at or before the caret, or -1 if there isn't one.
(Appendix A.)<P>

<A NAME=S4></A>
<H2>Drawing, and the caret</H2>
<PRE>
  draw() {
    const g = this.g, lines = this.lines();
    const { n, x } = this.where(lines);
    // scroll so the caret's line can be seen
    if (n * LINE &lt; this.scroll) this.scroll = n * LINE;
    if ((n + 1) * LINE &gt; this.scroll + this.h - 2 * PAD) {
      this.scroll = (n + 1) * LINE - this.h + 2 * PAD;
    }
    g.fillStyle = '#ffffff';
    g.fillRect(0, 0, this.w, this.h);
    g.strokeStyle = '#999999';
    g.strokeRect(0.5, 0.5, this.w - 1, this.h - 1);
    g.fillStyle = '#000000';
    g.font = this.font;
    g.textBaseline = 'middle';
    lines.forEach((l, i) =&gt; {
      const y = PAD + i * LINE - this.scroll + LINE / 2;
      g.fillText(this.text.slice(l.start, l.end), PAD, y);
    });
    if (this.focused) {
      wimp.setCaret(this.win, null, -1, { x: this.x + PAD + x,
        y: this.y + PAD + n * LINE - this.scroll, h: LINE });
    }
  }
</PRE>
First the box makes sure the caret can be seen: if its line is above the top, or below the bottom, of what's
showing, <CODE>scroll</CODE> changes so it isn't. Every line is then drawn <CODE>scroll</CODE> pixels higher
than it would be; the ones outside the box simply don't show on the canvas.<P>
The caret is the Wimp's own. <CODE>wimp.setCaret(win, null, -1, { x, y, h })</CODE> gives the window the input
focus with no writable icon, and draws the red caret at <CODE>x</CODE>, <CODE>y</CODE> in the work area,
<CODE>h</CODE> pixels high. It's the same call !JsEdit and !Edit use. Because the window then has the input
focus, the keys come to it - and so to the box's key handler, first.<P>
<CODE>focus()</CODE> marks the box as having the caret and draws it. When the caret goes to another window,
the window gets a <CODE>'losecaret'</CODE> event, and the box notes that it no longer has it.<P>

<A NAME=S5></A>
<H2>Clicks</H2>
<PRE>
  /** A click inside the box puts the caret there. */
  click(ev) {
    const inside = ev.x &gt;= this.x &amp;&amp; ev.x &lt; this.x + this.w &amp;&amp;
      ev.y &gt;= this.y &amp;&amp; ev.y &lt; this.y + this.h;
    if (!inside) { this.focused = false; return undefined; }
    const lines = this.lines();
    const n = Math.min(lines.length - 1, Math.max(0,
      Math.floor((ev.y - this.y - PAD + this.scroll) / LINE)));
    this.caret = this.nearest(lines[n], ev.x - this.x - PAD);
    this.focus();
    return true;
  }
  /** The place in a line nearest to x. */
  nearest(l, x) {
    this.g.font = this.font;
    for (let i = l.start; i &lt; l.end; i++) {
      const w = this.g.measureText(this.text.slice(l.start, i + 1))
        .width;
      if (w &gt; x) return i;
    }
    return l.end;
  }
</PRE>
A click outside the box isn't ours: <CODE>focused</CODE> becomes false and the handler returns
<CODE>undefined</CODE>, so the organiser gets the click. A click inside works out which line was clicked, from
the height of a line and how far it has scrolled, then <CODE>nearest</CODE> measures the line's text a character at a
time to find the character the pointer is over, and puts the caret before it. (So it always rounds to the
left: clicking on the right half of a letter puts the caret before it, not after. Comparing with the middle
of each character instead would put it on the nearer side.)<P>

<A NAME=S6></A>
<H2>Keys</H2>
<PRE>
  /** Put s in at the caret (or take n characters away before it). */
  insert(s, n = 0) {
    const at = this.caret - n;
    this.text = this.text.slice(0, at) + s +
      this.text.slice(this.caret);
    this.caret = at + s.length;
    this.draw();
    this.emit('change', { text: this.text });
  }

  key(ev) {
    if (!this.focused) return undefined;
    const lines = this.lines(), { n, x } = this.where(lines);
    const upDown = (d) =&gt; {
      const l = lines[n + d];
      if (l) this.caret = this.nearest(l, x);
    };
    switch (ev.code) {
      case 0x18C: this.caret = Math.max(0, this.caret - 1); break;
      case 0x18D:
        this.caret = Math.min(this.text.length, this.caret + 1);
        break;
      case 0x18F: upDown(-1); break;                        // up
      case 0x18E: upDown(1); break;                         // down
      case 8: case 127: if (this.caret) this.insert('', 1);
        return true;
      case 13: this.insert('\n'); return true;             // Return
      default:
        if (ev.code &gt;= 32 &amp;&amp; ev.code &lt; 256 &amp;&amp; !ev.ctrl) {
          this.insert(String.fromCharCode(ev.code));
          return true;
        }
        return undefined;                  // not ours: F3 and so on
    }
    this.draw();
    return true;
  }
</PRE>
All editing goes through <CODE>insert(s, n)</CODE>: take <CODE>n</CODE> characters away before the caret,
put <CODE>s</CODE> in, move the caret after it, redraw, and emit <CODE>'change'</CODE>. Typing is
<CODE>insert('a')</CODE>, Backspace is <CODE>insert('', 1)</CODE> and Return is <CODE>insert('\n')</CODE>.
Having only one way to change the text keeps it easy to get right.<P>
Left and Right move the caret one character. Up and Down move to the line above or below, as near as possible
to the same distance across - that's why <CODE>where()</CODE> works out <CODE>x</CODE>.<P>
Most important is the last line of <CODE>key</CODE>'s <CODE>default</CODE>: any key the box doesn't use
returns <CODE>undefined</CODE>. F3, Page Up, Ctrl-D and the rest then go on to the organiser, so saving,
turning pages and inserting the date still work while you're typing a note. (A text area that took every key
would be a trap.)<P>

<A NAME=S7></A>
<H2>Swapping it in</H2>
The Notepad changes in just two places: it imports <CODE>TextBox</CODE> from the application's directory
instead of <CODE>TextArea</CODE> from the desktop -<P>
<PRE>
import { TextBox } from './TextBox';
</PRE>
- and makes one where it used to make a <CODE>TextArea</CODE>:<P>
<PRE>
  // ------------------------------------------------ the text box
  /** Where the page's text goes on the right-hand page. */
  get rect() {
    const R = this.org.pages[1];
    return { x: R.x + 12, y: R.y + TOP - 4, w: R.w - 24,
      h: R.h - TOP - 36 };
  }
  show() {
    const p = this.doc.notes.get(this.page);
    if (!p || this.area) return;
    this.area = new TextBox(this.org.win, { ...this.rect,
      text: p.text, font: '14px Homerton' });
    this.area.on('change', ({ text }) =&gt; {
      this.doc.notes.update(p.id, { text });
    });
  }
  hide() { this.area?.remove(); this.area = null; }
  open(id) {
    this.hide();
    this.page = id;
    this.show();
    this.area?.focus();
    this.org.redraw();
  }
</PRE>
Everything else is the same, because <CODE>TextBox</CODE> has the same shape as <CODE>TextArea</CODE>:
<CODE>new TextBox(win, { x, y, w, h, text })</CODE>, <CODE>.text</CODE>, <CODE>focus()</CODE>,
<CODE>insert()</CODE>, <CODE>remove()</CODE> and the <CODE>'change'</CODE> event. Programming to a shape
rather than to one particular module is what makes a swap like this possible - the Organiser's sections
(chapter 10) work the same way.<P>
Ctrl-D still works because of the box's <CODE>undefined</CODE>: Ctrl-D isn't a key the box types in, so it
reaches the Notepad's own key handler, which inserts the date through the box:<P>
<PRE>
  // ------------------------------------------------ mouse and keys
  // (the TextBox sees clicks in it and keys while it has the caret
  // before the window does, so only the rest come here)
  click(ev) {
    const box = this.at(ev.x, ev.y);
    if (box) { this.open(box.ref.id); return true; }
    return false;
  }
  key(ev) {
    if (!this.area?.focused || ev.code !== 4) return false;  // Ctrl-D
    this.area.insert(D.format(D.today(), '%ZDY %MO %CE%YR'));
    return true;
  }
</PRE>

<H3>Trying it</H3>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1  Double-click <CODE>!Org17</CODE> in <CODE>$.Examples.JSApps</CODE>, then open the sample file
<CODE>Diary</CODE> with it.<P>
<DT><DD>2  Click the Notepad tab, then a title on the contents page, and type. Type a long line to see it
wrap, and enough lines to see it scroll.<P>
<DT><DD>3  Press Ctrl-D, then F3: the date goes in, and the file is saved.<P>
</DL>

<A NAME=S8></A>
<H2>What the desktop's TextArea does more</H2>
<CODE>TextBox</CODE> is about 160 lines. The desktop's <CODE>TextArea</CODE>, part of the desktop's own
source code (about 200 lines, and not on the disc), is built the same way, and adds:<P>
<UL>
<LI>More keys: Home and Ctrl-Left (start of the line), Ctrl-Right and Shift-Copy (end of the line),
Ctrl-Up and Ctrl-Down (start and end of the text), Copy (delete to the right), and Ctrl-U (delete the
line).<P>
<LI>Pasted text: it listens for the window's <CODE>'paste'</CODE> event.<P>
<LI>Moving between fields: Tab and Shift-Tab go to the next writable icon or text area in the window, as in
any dialogue box, and the Wimp's Tab from a writable icon comes to it. For that, the text areas in a window
are kept together (<CODE>win._textAreas</CODE>), and only one of them has the caret at a time.<P>
<LI>Losing the caret properly: it also watches <CODE>'caretmove'</CODE>, when the caret moves to an icon in
the same window.<P>
<LI>A <CODE>readOnly</CODE> setting, drawn in grey.<P>
<LI>Sharp text on high-resolution screens: its canvas has more pixels than its size on the screen.<P>
</UL>
None of these is hard; each is a few lines in the same pattern. That's the point of building one: nothing the
desktop gives you is magic, and when it doesn't give you something - a list, a form, a toolbar, a binder,
a text box - you now know how to make it.<P>

<A NAME=S9></A>
<H2>Where to go next</H2>
You've finished both books. Some places to go from here:<P>
<UL>
<LI><B>Make something of your own.</B> The best next step. Start small, plan it the way chapter 1 did, and
borrow freely from !Contacts and !Organiser - the toolkit modules were written to be reused.<P>
<LI><B>The RISC OS Style Guide</B> (Acorn, 1993; RISC OS Open publish a revised edition) is the reference for
how a RISC OS application should look and behave. Appendix D is a checklist drawn from it.<P>
<LI><B>The RISC OS Programmer's Reference Manuals</B> describe everything the real Wimp does, in the terms of
the original machine. This desktop's interface (appendix C, and book 1's appendix B) is modelled on it, so
much of what they say applies.<P>
<LI><B>JavaScript</B> goes much further than these books have taken it. A reference such as MDN Web Docs
(developer.mozilla.org) describes every part of the language; the language features appendix A lists are a
good place to start reading.<P>
<LI><B>The desktop's own applications</B> are JavaScript too, in the desktop's source code rather than on
the disc. Once you can read !Contacts, you can read them: !Edit, !Draw, !Paint and the rest show how bigger
programs are put together.<P>
</UL>
Happy programming.<P>

<A NAME=S10></A>
<H2>Summary</H2>
<UL>
<LI>A text area remembers its text, a caret index and a scroll position, and works out everything else -
the lines, the caret's position - when it needs it.<P>
<LI>It draws on its own canvas inside the window's work area, and shows the Wimp's caret with
<CODE>wimp.setCaret(win, null, -1, { x, y, h })</CODE>.<P>
<LI>Its handlers are added with <CODE>{ first: true }</CODE> so they run before the program's, and return
<CODE>undefined</CODE> for anything not meant for it. <CODE>remove()</CODE> takes them away again.<P>
<LI>All changes go through one method, <CODE>insert</CODE>, which emits <CODE>'change'</CODE>.<P>
<LI>Modules with the same shape can be swapped for one another.<P>
</UL>

<A NAME=S11></A>
<H2>Exercises</H2>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1  Make Home move to the start of the caret's line, and Copy delete the character to the right of
the caret.<P>
<DT><DD>2  Add Ctrl-U, which deletes the whole of the caret's line.<P>
<DT><DD>3  Let text be pasted into the box.<P>
<DT><DD>4  Scroll the box with the mouse wheel. (Windows have a <CODE>'wheel'</CODE> event with
<CODE>dy</CODE>, the amount to scroll; a handler returning <CODE>true</CODE> stops the window scrolling
itself.)<P>
<DT><DD>5  Harder: a selection. Shift with Left or Right extends a selection from the caret; typing replaces
it; it's drawn with a coloured background.<P>
</DL>

<A NAME=S12></A>
<H2>Answers</H2>
<DL>
<DT><DD>1  Two more cases in <CODE>key</CODE>, before <CODE>default</CODE>:
<PRE>
case 30: this.caret = lines[n].start; break;            // Home
case 0x18B:                                             // Copy
  if (this.caret &lt; this.text.length) {
    this.caret++;
    this.insert('', 1);
  }
  return true;
</PRE>
Copy moves the caret past the character and deletes it as Backspace would.<P>
<DT><DD>2  Find the line's ends in the text itself (not the wrapped lines), and cut it out:
<PRE>
case 21: {                                              // Ctrl-U
  const t = this.text;
  const a = t.lastIndexOf('\n', this.caret - 1) + 1;
  let b = t.indexOf('\n', this.caret);
  b = b &lt; 0 ? t.length : b + 1;
  this.caret = b;
  this.insert('', b - a);
  return true;
}
</PRE>
<DT><DD>3  Listen for the window's <CODE>'paste'</CODE> event, first, while the box has the caret, and keep
its <CODE>off</CODE> with the others:
<PRE>
win.on('paste', (ev) =&gt; {
  if (!this.focused) return undefined;
  this.insert(ev.text.replace(/\r\n?/g, '\n'));
  return true;
}, { first: true }),
</PRE>
<DT><DD>4  The <CODE>'wheel'</CODE> event doesn't say where the pointer is, so take it while the box has the
caret: in the constructor,
<PRE>
win.on('wheel', (ev) =&gt; {
  if (!this.focused) return undefined;
  const most = this.lines().length * LINE - this.h + 2 * PAD;
  this.scroll = Math.max(0, Math.min(most,
    this.scroll + ev.dy));
  this.drawOnly();
  return true;
}, { first: true }),
</PRE>
- but <CODE>draw</CODE> scrolls back to the caret, so split it: a <CODE>drawOnly()</CODE> that draws at the
current <CODE>scroll</CODE>, called by <CODE>draw</CODE> after it has made the caret visible. While the box
doesn't have the caret, the wheel scrolls the window as usual.<P>
<DT><DD>5  Keep a second index, <CODE>anchor</CODE>, where the selection started (or
<CODE>null</CODE>). Shift-Left and Shift-Right (<CODE>0x19C</CODE> and <CODE>0x19D</CODE>) set
<CODE>anchor</CODE> if it's <CODE>null</CODE> and move the caret; other movement clears it. In
<CODE>draw</CODE>, fill the background from <CODE>Math.min(anchor, caret)</CODE> to
<CODE>Math.max(anchor, caret)</CODE> on each line it covers, before drawing the text. In
<CODE>insert</CODE>, if there's a selection, remove it first. It's the biggest of these exercises - the
desktop's own !Edit has a whole module for selections.<P>
</DL>

